WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.163
Intelligence coordination depends on source discipline.

00:00:02.163 --> 00:00:06.290
A source may be internal telemetry, a public report, a partner tip,

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.760
a vendor advisory, a fraud record, a case note, a sanitized incident summary,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.480
or a regulated reporting channel.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.262
Different sources have different reliability, credibility, access limits, and handling rules.

00:00:16.262 --> 00:00:20.389
Treating all sources as equal leads to poor analysis and damaged trust.

00:00:20.389 --> 00:00:23.828
Provenance records where information came from and how it moved.

00:00:23.828 --> 00:00:26.579
Analysts should document when the information was received,

00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:30.705
who provided it or which system produced it, what transformations were performed,

00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:35.176
what limitations apply, and whether any caveats or handling restrictions came with it.

00:00:35.176 --> 00:00:39.646
If a source was summarized, redacted, aggregated, normalized, or combined with another dataset,

00:00:39.646 --> 00:00:42.397
that transformation should be visible to authorized reviewers.

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:45.492
Source reliability and information credibility are related but separate.

00:00:45.492 --> 00:00:48.931
A generally reliable partner can pass along an unconfirmed observation.

00:00:48.931 --> 00:00:53.401
A public report can be accurate on one point and incomplete on another.

00:00:53.401 --> 00:00:57.872
Internal telemetry can be precise but limited by logging gaps or retention windows.

00:00:57.872 --> 00:01:01.999
Analysts should describe what the source supports, what it does not support,

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:04.750
and how confident they are in the interpretation.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.876
Handling rules control who can see information, how it can be stored,

00:01:08.876 --> 00:01:12.659
how long it can be retained, whether it can cross borders,

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:14.378
whether it can be quoted,

00:01:14.378 --> 00:01:17.817
and whether it can be shared only as a summary.

00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:22.631
Some partner information may be usable for internal analysis but not for external dissemination.

00:01:22.631 --> 00:01:26.414
Some sensitive records may require access controls, markings, or legal review.

00:01:26.414 --> 00:01:30.884
Some details may need to be removed before sharing with a broader audience.

00:01:30.884 --> 00:01:33.292
Respecting handling rules protects accuracy and relationships.

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:37.418
A partner that shares a limited warning may stop sharing if the

00:01:37.418 --> 00:01:39.138
information is redistributed without permission.

00:01:39.138 --> 00:01:44.296
A privacy team may reject a product that includes identifiers not needed for the decision.

00:01:44.296 --> 00:01:49.110
A regulator or customer may need carefully caveated findings rather than raw working notes.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.237
Provenance and handling discipline help the team use information responsibly while preserving

00:01:53.237 --> 00:01:55.300
the trust that makes coordination possible.
